00:13:52.030 --> 00:14:06.440
Mark Blackman: is describing the block structure and the hashing logic of the block. Not logic, but how it's hashed. And really, there are two major points to this hip.

78
00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:17.520
Mark Blackman: The first of all is we are adding some extra bits… to enable…

79
00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:22.070
Mark Blackman: to prevent what's called a pre-image attack, so…

80
00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:31.399
Mark Blackman: In… in the Merkle tree that makes up the database, the state database of the consensus node.

81
00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:41.779
Mark Blackman: There is a chance that a node with, can either have one leaf, Or two leaves. Two children.

82
00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:54.589
Mark Blackman: And, obviously, if you get to the end of the tree, as the tree is being built out, you'll have a situation where a node will only have one parent.

83
00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:57.420
Mark Blackman: And initially, in the design.

84
00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:04.219
Mark Blackman: We just had it, basically a node in either one or two of these children's.

85
00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:08.670
Mark Blackman: However there's an attack, because if you think about it.

86
00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:14.939
Mark Blackman: If a node has one parent with a certain hash value.

87
00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:19.620
Mark Blackman: There is a way that an attacker could use

88
00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:31.409
Mark Blackman: two different values and achieve the same hash, because that extra node is available, and if they put the right value in that second node.

89
00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:35.740
Mark Blackman: And then change the value of that first

90
00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:50.749
Mark Blackman: I'm sorry, change the value of the second child, and then change the value of the first child. It is possible that you could have two children make the same hash value as one child.

91
00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:58.070
Mark Blackman: And so, to prevent this, we have put a prefix, which identifies

92
00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:02.460
Mark Blackman: the type, it is, so… if it…

93
00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:10.589
Mark Blackman: is, just a leaf node. We'll have the prefix of 00. If it's an internal node with one child.

94
00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:21.210
Mark Blackman: then it is, 01, and if is a node with 2 children, 02. So, by stating,

95
00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:25.529
Mark Blackman: The actual population of this part of the tree

96
00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:31.480
Mark Blackman: We could prevent somebody going in and, trying this pre-image attack.

97
00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:33.090
Mark Blackman: So, if…

98
00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:41.260
Mark Blackman: If the state only has one child, this prefix will say one child, and therefore, if somebody tries to

99
00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:46.289
Mark Blackman: Edits the tree with two different values.

100
00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:52.050
Mark Blackman: That prefix would have to change, and that would definitely change the hashing,

101
00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:57.299
Mark Blackman: the hash value of that. So it's effectively a security

102
00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:01.820
Mark Blackman: Technique we're putting into the structure here.

103
00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:03.519
Mark Blackman: the block structure.

104
00:17:04.119 --> 00:17:06.189
Mark Blackman: Any questions on that?

105
00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:11.010
Mark Blackman: Okay.

106
00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:18.539
Mark Blackman: And then the second aspect of this is really talking about how the block is structured, logically.

107
00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:22.960
Mark Blackman: And this is really, to enable us to…

108
00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:33.289
Mark Blackman: major part of the block will be a subtree. You can think of it as a subtree within the tree, and then that would have a root, so…

